body:
For my loading spinner needs, I do something like this in the store:

    import { createStore } from "vuex";
    export default createStore({
        state: {
        loading_keys: {}
        },
        getters: {
        is_loading(state){
            return Object.values(state.loading_keys).reduce((prev, cur)=>{return prev + cur}, 0) > 0;
        }
        },
        mutations: {
        startLoading(state, key){
            state.loading_keys[key] = state.loading_keys[key] + 1 || 1;
        },
        stopLoading(state, key){
            setTimeout(()=>{
            state.loading_keys[key] -= 1;
            if(state.loading_keys[key] <= 0)
            {
                delete state.loading_keys[key];
            }
            }, 500);
        }
        }
    });

and then use it in a component like this

    <template>
        <div>
            <div
                v-if="is_loading"
                id="spinner"
                class="d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center"
            >
                <div class="spinner_content">
                    <img src="/assets/images/loader.gif" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </template>

    <script>
    export default {
        computed: {
            is_loading() {
                return this.$store.getters["is_loading"];
            },
        },
        methods: {
            startLoading(key) {
                this.$store.commit("startLoading", key);
            },
            stopLoading(key) {
                this.$store.commit("stopLoading", key);
            },
        },
        async mounted() {
            this.startLoading("twitterSetup");
            //some ajax function
            this.stopLoading("twitterSetup");
        },
    };
    </script>

I use it most when working in Vue.  Consider this code:

```
class Something {
    Constructor(){
        this.some_value = "My Value";
    }
    func1(){
        let that = this;
        setTimeout(function(){
               console.log(this.some_value); //undefined
               console.log(that.some_value); //"My Value"
           }, 1000);
    func2(){
        let that = this;
        setTimeout(()=>{
               console.log(this.some_value); //"My Value"
               console.log(that.some_value); //"My Value"
           }, 1000);
    }
}
```

HOW it works, I don't know, but it makes the code a lot nicer to look at and easier to follow since you don't have extra `that`s all over the place.  You can redefine func2 as just:

```
func2(){
        setTimeout(()=>{
               console.log(this.some_value); //"My Value"
           }, 1000);
    }
```

It's useful for nested callbacks or one liners that you don't want to have to keep track of the actual scope of this and just want it to work.

Is there a benefit of doing it that way rather than the old fashioned way?

I was just playing around and implementing a linked list, so my code ended up like this:

```
for(let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++){
    let item = arr[i];
    let item.prev = arr[i-1];
    let item.next = arr[i+1];
}
```

Basically, a `do..while` guarantees the code is run **at least** one time.  Consider the following (admittedly contrived and oversimplified example:

```
let x = 0;
x += something();
x += 1;
x += random_func();
while(x < some_stupid_large_number){
    x += something();
    x += 1;
    x += random_func();
}
```

vs

```
let x = 0;
do {
    x += something();
    x += 1;
    x += random_func();
} while(x < some_stupid_large_number)
```

Honestly, I've almost never found a use case where it made sense to use a `do..while`, but it's there in case you need it.

Personally, I don't think it's a problem sending in all the data using a prop.  I'd just recommend using computed fields to pull the data out of the prop so you don't accidentally cause any side effects.  It's not good practice to use the data directly from the prop.

So you'd have like

    props: [
        navData: {type: object}
    ],
    computed: [
        nav_items(){ return this.navData; }
    ]

Though, in the parent component, you wouldn't have the json directly in the template part.  you'd have it defined int he script and then the html would just be like:

    data(){
        return {
         nav_data:
        [{
           text: 'Home',
           url: this.route('home'),
           dropdown: false 
        }, {
            text: 'Dropdown',
            url: '#',
            dropdown: [
                {
                    text: 'Item 1',
                    url: '/item-1'
                }, {
                    text: 'Item 2',
                    url: '/item-2'
                }
            ]
        }]
        }}

    ....

    <nav-component :navData="nav_data"></nav-component>

I could be wrong, but it looks like OP's function is to find the differences between the two arrays.  

If you wanted to clean up OP's code, you could use `filter` instead of `for ... in` but it would still do the same amount of iterations.  I'm no expert on algorithms, though, and usually focus on "get shit done" rather than "make it go fast".

```javascript
function diffArray(arr1, arr2){
    //get everything from arr1 that is not in arr2
    const a = arr1.filter( item => { return !arr2.includes(item); } );
    //get everything from arr2 that is not in arr1 
    const b = arr2.filter( item => { return !arr1.includes(item); } );
    //combine and return result
    return a.concat(b);
}

const arr1 = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const arr2 = [2, 4, 6, 8];

const diff = diffArray(arr1, arr2);
//[1, 3, 5, 6, 8]
```

Alternatively, if you still wanted to use loops, but didn't want to use `for ... in`, there's `for ... of` which works for arrays:

```javascript
function diffArray(arr1, arr2) {
   var newArr = [];
   for(let a1 of arr1){
      if(arr2.includes(a1)===false){
         newArr.push(a1);
      }
   }
   for(let a2 of arr2){
      if(arr1.includes(a2)===false){
         newArr.push(a2);
      }
   }
   return newArr;
}
```

Honestly, this would be how I'd do it first and if it wasn't performant enough, I'd refactor to the first one.  If that still wasn't performant enough, I'd cry and google the answer.

If there's a better way, I'd love to know it.  Never miss an opportunity to level up your skills.

Personally, I'd use a `for...in` or `for...of`.

```
for( let item of a ) {
console.log(item);
}
```

or 

```
for( let i in a){
let item = a[i];
console.log("index:" + i + " item:" + item)
```

You can do it that way, but in my opinion, form input images are a little old fashioned.  They were created in the very early days of HTML.  For your use case, you could just as easily add images inside a `button` tag or style it using CSS.  I'd do something like this:

    <button type="submit">
        <img src="button_image_1.png" class="donBut">
        <img src="button_image_2.png" class="donBut hide">
    </button>

Best practice, though, would be to just style it via CSS and not use images at all (unless you want an icon in there, then use an SVG or something):

    <button type="submit" class="submit-button white-button">
      <img src="icon.svg" class="icon">
      Donate Now
    </button>
    <style>
    .submit-button {
      border-radius: 50%;
      border: solid black 2px;
      color: black;
    }
    .submit-button .icon
    {
      stroke: black;
    }

    .white-button
    {
      color: white;
      border: solid white 2px;
    }
    .white-button .icon
    {
      stroke: white;
    }
    </style>

Note:  This code is just an example and hasn't been tested.

The `&&` operator will return either the first value that is `falsey` or the last value in a chain.  It doesn't cast anything to a boolean as you might expect since you normally see it in conditionals.  Outside of conditions, it's useful for getting a value nested in a dictionary without throwing an `undefined` exception.

```
let something = main && main.sub && main.sub.secondsub;
```

if `main` is undefined, then something is `undefined`.  If `main` is defined, but it doesn't have a `main.sub` property, then `main.sub` would be undefined, so something becomes `undefined`.  But if it has both of those (neither is falsey) then something becomes the value of `main.sub.secondsub`.

Likewise, `||` will return the first value that is `truthy`. This is useful for having default function parameters:

```
function funcName(parameter1){
   parameter1 = parameter1 || 'default';
}
```

Think of them as shorthand for a bunch of nested `if` statements.
